gs-hero-output\"><img src=\"/portfolio/case-assets/gensound-preview.webp\" width=\"934\" height=\"459\" alt=\"Actual GenSound output from the Chopin example: concentric rings and a Hilbert arrangement\"><figcaption>ONE ANALYSIS / TWO WAYS TO SEE IT<br>CHOPIN / NOCTURNE OP. 9 NO. 2</figcaption></figure></section><section id=\"gs-lab\" class=\"gs-lab\" aria-label=\"genSound Color Lab\"><iframe scrolling=\"no\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"/experiments/gensound/index.html\" data-gensound-frame data-gensound-src=\"/experiments/gensound/index.html\" title=\"genSound Color Lab — audio into generative art\" allow=\"fullscreen; clipboard-write\" allowfullscreen></iframe><p class=\"gs-loading\" data-gensound-loading role=\"status\">Loading the color lab…</p></section><section id=\"gs-method\" class=\"gs-method\" aria-labelledby=\"gs-method-title\"><div class=\"gs-method-intro\"><span class=\"gs-eyebrow\">HOW IT WORKS</span><h2 id=\"gs-method-title\">A whole track.<br><em>A single field.</em></h2><p>The lab measures sound, maps the measurements to color, then places the same moments in two different arrangements.</p></div><div class=\"gs-method-steps\"><article><span>01 / MEASURE</span><h3>Overlapping moments.</h3><p>250,000 windows, each a tenth of a second long. Each window records the note, loudness and strongest frequency.</p></article><article><span>02 / MAP</span><h3>Measurements become color.</h3><p>By default, note chooses hue, loudness drives saturation and strongest frequency sets lightness. Change the rules or palette in the lab. Silence stays black.</p></article><article><span>03 / ARRANGE</span><h3>Time takes a shape.</h3><p>Rings grow outward from the opening. The Hilbert layout keeps nearby moments together. Both show the same analysis.</p></article></div></section><section class=\"gs-credits\" aria-label=\"GenSound source and recording credits\"><div><span class=\"gs-eyebrow\">SOURCE / RECORDINGS / CREDITS</span><a href=\"https://github.com/joshcoledesign/genSound\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View GenSound source ↗</a></div><p>Example recordings: Chopin, Nocturne Op. 9 No. 2, performed by Peter Johnston via Musopen (CC0); Beethoven, Symphony No. 8, iv. Allegro vivace, Musopen recording (public domain); “Hitman” by Kevin MacLeod (incompetech.com), licensed under <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>. The recordings are not hosted here.</p><nav aria-label=\"GenSound credits and licenses\"><a href=\"/experiments/gensound/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open lab in its own tab ↗</a><a href=\"/experiments/gensound/EXAMPLE_TRACK_CREDITS.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Recording credits ↗</a><a href=\"/experiments/gensound/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Third-party notices ↗</a><a href=\"/experiments/gensound/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">License ↗</a></nav></section><span hidden data-gensound-signature=\"/portfolio/media/552cafea09114d2583fa.svg\"></span></main><footer class=\"rd-footer\"><span>JOSH COLE / EXPERIMENTAL WORK</span><a href=\"/#experiments\" data-return-home class=\"cursor-interaction\">Back to all experiments ↗</a></footer>\r\n </section>\r\n\r\n<div id=\"gprs-below-the-surface\" hidden aria-label=\"GPRS SiteMap — Below the surface\">\r\n <header class=\"sx-header\"><a href=\"/\" data-logo-home class=\"case-home-logo cursor-interaction\" aria-label=\"Josh Cole — home\"><img data-signature width=\"94\" height=\"52\" alt=\"Josh Cole signature\"></a><a href=\"/#case-studies\" data-return-case class=\"sx-case-return cursor-interaction\">← Case studies</a><a href=\"#sx-story\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Explore the story ↓</a></header>\r\n <main>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-hero\" aria-labelledby=\"sx-title\">\r\n   <img class=\"sx-hero-map\" data-image=\"map-lines\" width=\"960\" height=\"373\" alt=\"SiteMap workspace: captured utility lines layered over a real site\">\r\n   <div class=\"sx-hero-top\"><div class=\"case-client-brand\"><img class=\"case-client-logo\" src=\"/portfolio/media/a910174bc180be30dcb0.webp\" width=\"800\" height=\"638\" alt=\"GPRS logo\"><div class=\"case-client-meta\"><span>GPRS / SITEMAP</span><span>2023–2024</span></div></div><span>DESIGN &amp; DIRECTION<br>JOSH COLE</span></div>\r\n   <h1 id=\"sx-title\">SiteMap<span>.</span></h1>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-hero-bottom\"><p>What’s below<br>changes everything.</p><span>A MAP-FIRST FIELD PLATFORM<br>DESKTOP / TABLET / MOBILE</span></div>\r\n   <span class=\"sx-hero-coordinate\" aria-hidden=\"true\">SURFACE / CONTEXT / CONNECTION</span>\r\n  </section>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-interaction sx-pad\" aria-labelledby=\"sx-interaction-title\">\r\n <div class=\"sx-interaction-heading\"><div><span class=\"sx-label\">THE CORE INTERACTION</span><h2 id=\"sx-interaction-title\">Open the record.<br><em>Keep the place.</em></h2></div><div class=\"sx-interaction-action\"><button type=\"button\" data-toggle-panel aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"sx-record-panel\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Open line detail ↗</button><span data-panel-status role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">The map is your workspace.</span></div></div>\r\n <figure><div class=\"sx-interaction-canvas\"><img class=\"sx-interaction-map\" data-image=\"map-lines\" width=\"960\" height=\"373\" alt=\"Utility records on the SiteMap map workspace\"><div id=\"sx-record-panel\" class=\"sx-record-panel\" data-record-panel aria-hidden=\"true\" inert><img data-image=\"line-panel\" width=\"346\" height=\"744\" alt=\"Actual Sewer Panel interface with work-order details and the colored condition rail\"></div><span class=\"sx-context-label\" aria-hidden=\"true\">MAP CONTEXT / RETAINED</span></div><figcaption><span>Interaction study using actual interface captures.</span><span>Open a detail. The site stays in view.</span></figcaption></figure>\r\n</section>\r\n\r\n<section id=\"sx-story\" class=\"sx-opening sx-pad\">\r\n   <div class=\"sx-opening-top\"><span class=\"sx-label\">THE ENGAGEMENT</span><p class=\"sx-role\">Lead UX/UI Designer<br>2023–2024</p><p class=\"sx-role\">One designer.<br>Two development teams.<br>Three surfaces.</p></div>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-brief\"><div><h2 data-source-id=\"source-1\">In brief</h2><ul><li data-source-id=\"source-2\"><a href=\"#gprs-the-shape-of-the-work\" data-chapter-link=\"the-shape-of-the-work\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">The shape of the work</a>: a live product, unified and extended at the same time.</li><li data-source-id=\"source-3\"><a href=\"#gprs-how-the-product-thinks\" data-chapter-link=\"how-the-product-thinks\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">How the product thinks</a>: the map is the surface, color carries condition, tags instead of folders.</li><li data-source-id=\"source-4\"><a href=\"#gprs-what-i-built\" data-chapter-link=\"what-i-built\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">What I built</a>: every interface across desktop, tablet, and mobile.</li><li data-source-id=\"source-5\"><a href=\"#gprs-what-i-take-from-it\" data-chapter-link=\"what-i-take-from-it\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">What I take from it</a>: redesigning a live product is its own discipline.</li></ul></div><div class=\"sx-body\"><p class=\"cs-opening\" data-source-id=\"source-6\">Ground Penetrating Radar Systems scans what's underneath a job site (gas, water, electric, sewer, telecom) before anyone digs. SiteMap is the software product where all of that data lives. It's a map-first interface where building owners, contractors, and engineers can see the utilities and features GPRS captured, drill into work orders, manage their properties over time, and trust that what's on screen matches what's in the ground. A high volume of records, scans, and assets flows through the system, across desktop, tablet, and mobile.</p></div></div>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-introduction\"><span class=\"sx-label\">MY ROLE</span><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-8\">I joined SiteMap as Lead UX/UI Designer in 2023 for the redesign. The new version shipped in late 2024 and continued evolving from there as new functionality came online. I was the only UX/UI designer on the product, and the design director for it. Every interface across desktop, tablet, and mobile came from my desk. The teams around me were product managers, a business analyst, and two development teams, one for web and one for mobile, each with its own dev lead and engineers. I ran a weekly cadence with both to keep what they were building aligned with the UX, and to work through the design implications of decisions they were making.</p></div></div>\r\n  </section>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-challenge sx-pad\" id=\"gprs-the-shape-of-the-work\">\r\n   <div class=\"sx-section-label\"><span>01 / THE CHALLENGE</span><h2 data-source-id=\"source-9\">The shape of the work</h2></div>\r\n   <h2 class=\"sx-statement\">Rebuild it.<br>Keep it<br><em>running.</em></h2>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-two-column\"><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-10\">SiteMap was already in production when I came on. Customers were using it daily. The product had good ideas everywhere, but it had grown without a design system underneath it, and the work had splintered. Different surfaces handled the same patterns differently. The same data appeared in incompatible ways across the product. Adding new features meant inventing each one almost from scratch, because the foundation to build on was crumbling.</p></div><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-12\">The business wanted more, not less. A richer feature set, more granular permission levels, deeper subscription tiers, more of what users could do with the data. The redesign had to do two things at the same time. Unify what was already there, and make room for everything coming next, without breaking the workflows customers already depended on.</p></div></div>\r\n   <figure class=\"sx-subscriptions\"><div class=\"sx-wide-image\"><img data-image=\"subscription-mgmt\" width=\"960\" height=\"655\" alt=\"Subscription management and subscription details panels\"></div><figcaption><span data-source-id=\"source-11\">Subscription management and subscription details panels</span><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"subscription-mgmt\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">See the full screen ↗</button></figcaption></figure>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-tension\"><span class=\"sx-label\">THE TENSION</span><div class=\"sx-body\"><p class=\"cs-pull\" data-source-id=\"source-13\">That pull in two directions ran through every decision. A from-scratch redesign would have been easier in some ways. No legacy to honor, no users to protect. This was the harder version. Bring coherence to something that's already working, while adding complexity the product hadn't been built to support, and ship it without anyone feeling the floor shift underneath them.</p></div></div>\r\n  </section>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-principles\" id=\"gprs-how-the-product-thinks\">\r\n   <div class=\"sx-principles-intro sx-pad\"><div class=\"sx-section-label\"><span>02 / THE DESIGN LANGUAGE</span><h2 data-source-id=\"source-14\">How the product thinks</h2></div><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-15\">A few principles emerged from the redesign and held across the suite.</p></div></div>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-map-sequence\" data-map-stage>\r\n    <div class=\"sx-map-copy\"><span class=\"sx-label\">A PLACE TO WORK</span><h2>Keep your<br><em>bearings.</em></h2></div>\r\n    <img data-map-image data-image=\"map-lines\" width=\"960\" height=\"373\" alt=\"SiteMap map workspace with captured utility lines\">\r\n    <div class=\"sx-map-controls\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Inspect the map workspace\"><button type=\"button\" data-map-view=\"map-lines\" aria-pressed=\"true\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Workspace</button><button type=\"button\" data-map-view=\"historical-data-drawing\" aria-pressed=\"false\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Drawing tools</button><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"map-lines\" data-map-inspect class=\"cursor-interaction\">Full screen ↗</button></div>\r\n   </div>\r\n   <div class=\"sx-map-explanation sx-pad\"><div class=\"sx-body sx-principle\"><p class=\"cs-principle\" data-source-id=\"source-16\"><strong>The map is the surface.</strong> SiteMap's entire premise is that the map is the source of truth. The satellite view of the site, with utility lines and feature markers layered on top, is where the user lives. Panels slide in <em>over</em> the map rather than replacing it. Detail views keep the map's context. The reader never loses sight of where they are physically, because where they are physically is the whole point. Making the map the workspace was the design decision that everything else hung on.</p></div><figure class=\"sx-drawing\"><img data-image=\"historical-data-drawing\" width=\"960\" height=\"655\" alt=\"Historical utility data layered on the site map with drawing tools\"><figcaption><span data-source-id=\"source-7\">Historical utility data layered on the site map with drawing tools</span><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"historical-data-drawing\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Inspect ↗</button></figcaption></figure></div>\r\n   <section class=\"sx-condition sx-pad\">\r\n    <div class=\"sx-condition-title\"><span class=\"sx-label\">READ THE SIGNAL</span><h2>Read<br>the<br><em>rail.</em></h2><div class=\"sx-color-rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></div>\r\n    <figure class=\"sx-condition-work\"><div class=\"sx-condition-crop\"><img data-image=\"pipe-assessment\" width=\"960\" height=\"630\" alt=\"Detail of the pipe condition assessment and its colored rail\"></div><figcaption><span data-source-id=\"source-18\">Pipe condition assessment with the color-coded rail</span><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"pipe-assessment\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">See the full screen ↗</button></figcaption></figure>\r\n    <div class=\"sx-body sx-condition-body sx-principle\"><p class=\"cs-principle\" data-source-id=\"source-17\"><strong>Color carries condition.</strong> Storm lines run down a vertical rail with their pipe condition color-coded (green for excellent, yellow for fair, red for immediate attention, and so on). The user scanning a sewer system isn't reading words. They're reading the rail. The condition language is consistent across utility types and across surfaces, so a field engineer on a phone and an estimator at a desk see the same colors meaning the same things.</p></div>\r\n   </section>\r\n   <section class=\"sx-tags sx-pad\"><div class=\"sx-tags-title\"><span class=\"sx-label\">A SYSTEM OF CONNECTIONS</span><h2>Find it.<br>Without<br><em>the tree.</em></h2></div><div class=\"sx-body sx-principle\"><p class=\"cs-principle\" data-source-id=\"source-19\"><strong>Tag-based organization, not folder trees.</strong> The Plan Room, where CAD files, submittals, building plans, and site documents live, uses tags rather than a deep folder hierarchy. Tags carry color identity, audit metadata, and a description, so a customer can find what they need without remembering whose computer it was filed on. The same tag system surfaces in Folder Details and in the Show in Plan Room cross-reference, so it works everywhere the user might look.</p></div><figure class=\"sx-plan-room\"><img data-image=\"plan-room-details\" width=\"960\" height=\"655\" alt=\"Plan Room file details with tags\"><figcaption><span data-source-id=\"source-20\">Plan Room file details with tags</span><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"plan-room-details\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Inspect the work ↗</button></figcaption></figure></section>\r\n   <section class=\"sx-surfaces sx-pad\"><span class=\"sx-label\">THE SYSTEM UNDERNEATH</span><h2>Built for<br><em>the job.</em></h2><div class=\"sx-two-column\"><div class=\"sx-body sx-principle\"><p class=\"cs-principle\" data-source-id=\"source-21\"><strong>Performance is part of the design.</strong> A site can have thousands of features on the map at once. The Settings panel exposes the controls that make that workable (render scale, feature icon size, hide-removed-lines, low-memory mode) and the defaults are calibrated so the map stays usable without the user ever having to open the panel. The settings exist for the power user. The defaults exist for everyone else.</p></div><div class=\"sx-body sx-principle\"><p class=\"cs-principle\" data-source-id=\"source-22\"><strong>Same language on every device, not the same product.</strong> Desktop, tablet, and mobile share the same data model and the same language, but they don't try to be the same product. Desktop is where the heavy work happens (reviewing inspection videos, managing subscriptions, administering tags). Mobile is where the field happens (pulling up a line detail in the dirt, confirming a work order on site). Tablet sits between them, comfortable in both contexts. The design treats each surface as serious in its own right, not as a downsized version of the next one up.</p></div></div></section>\r\n  </section>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-scope sx-pad\" id=\"gprs-what-i-built\"><div class=\"sx-section-label\"><span>03 / THE SCOPE</span><h2 data-source-id=\"source-23\">What I built</h2></div><div class=\"sx-scope-word\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Connected.</div><div class=\"sx-body sx-scope-description\"><p data-source-id=\"source-24\">Across the redesign and the year of feature work that followed, the scope spanned the SiteMap canvas itself, the Plan Room file system, sewer and line detail with embedded inspection video and PACP observation codes, Reality Capture for point clouds and 3D scans, subscription management for the business customers, an admin surface covering tags, user management, training, and messaging, multi-tier permissions, the settings layer that controls map performance, and on-canvas annotation tools.</p></div><figure class=\"sx-tag-system\"><img data-image=\"tag-system\" width=\"960\" height=\"461\" alt=\"Tag administration screen\"><figcaption><span data-source-id=\"source-25\">Tag administration screen</span><button type=\"button\" data-inspect=\"tag-system\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">See the full screen ↗</button></figcaption></figure><div class=\"sx-body sx-scope-description\"><p data-source-id=\"source-26\">All of it on three surfaces, all of it tied to the same data model, all of it designed by one person working with strong dev teams on each platform. The weekly reviews with web and mobile were where the design held. A place for the engineers to surface what they'd built or where they were stuck, and a place to make the calls that kept the surfaces aligned without grinding development to a stop.</p></div></section>\r\n  <section class=\"sx-takeaway sx-pad\" id=\"gprs-what-i-take-from-it\"><div class=\"sx-section-label\"><span>04 / THE TAKEAWAY</span><h2 data-source-id=\"source-27\">What I take from it</h2></div><h2>A better tomorrow.<br><em>Without breaking<br>yesterday.</em></h2><div class=\"sx-body sx-takeaway-lead\"><p data-source-id=\"source-28\">Two things came out of this engagement that I've taken with me.</p></div><div class=\"sx-two-column\"><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-29\">The first is that <strong>redesigning a live product is a different discipline than designing a new one.</strong> A new product gets to be perfect on day one because nobody is using it yet. A live product has to be better tomorrow than it was today without breaking the work people did yesterday. Every decision is weighted by the people already inside the product. That weight is what makes the work matter.</p></div><div class=\"sx-body\"><p data-source-id=\"source-30\">The second is that <strong>design coherence at this scope is mostly an alignment problem, not a drawing problem.</strong> The screens come together when the surfaces are reasoning from the same model. Most of the work was making sure that model held, across desktop, tablet, and mobile, across feature areas, across the developers building each piece, so that everything visible on the screen was the visible end of a deeper consistency underneath.</p></div></div></section>\r\n  <footer class=\"sx-footer sx-pad\"><span>GPRS / SITEMAP<br>2023–2024</span><a href=\"/#case-studies\" data-return-case class=\"sx-case-return cursor-interaction\">← Back to case studies</a><a href=\"#sx-title\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Back to the surface ↑</a><span>DESIGN &amp; DIRECTION<br>JOSH COLE</span></footer>\r\n </main>\r\n <section class=\"sx-inspector\" hidden aria-label=\"Full project screen\"><div class=\"sx-inspector-header\"><div><span class=\"sx-label\">THE ACTUAL WORK</span><h2 data-inspector-title></h2></div><button type=\"button\" data-close-inspector class=\"cursor-interaction\">Back to the story ↙</button></div><img data-inspector-image alt=\"\" width=\"960\" height=\"655\"><p data-inspector-caption></p><div class=\"sx-inspector-context\" data-inspector-context></div></section>\r\n <script type=\"application/json\" data-case-assets>{\"map-lines\":{\"src\":\"/portfolio/media/8fc80550ac18f25af0c6.webp\",\"width\":960,\"height\":373,\"alt\":\"SiteMap map workspace with captured utility lines\",\"context\":\"\\u003cp>\\u003cstrong>The map is the surface.\\u003c/strong> SiteMap's entire premise is that the map is the source of truth. The satellite view of the site, with utility lines and feature markers layered on top, is where the user lives. Panels slide in \\u003cem>over\\u003c/em> the map rather than replacing it. Detail views keep the map's context. The reader never loses sight of where they are physically, because where they are physically is the whole point. Making the map the workspace was the design decision that everything else hung on.\\u003c/p>\",\"focus\":{\"x\":0.52,\"y\":0.5,\"zoom\":160}},\"historical-data-drawing\":{\"src\":\"/portfolio/media/7bc6795334e36c633da9.webp\",\"width\":960,\"height\":655,\"alt