 src=\"/portfolio/media/0848497b72c8bc51d913.png\" width=\"408\" height=\"408\" alt=\"Einstein Bros. Bagels logo\"><span class=\"eb-label\">ORDERING / REWARDS / THE STORE SIDE</span></div><h1>The usual.<br><em>Done right.</em></h1><p>Einstein Bros. Bagels App</p><div class=\"eb-role\">UX Director, Xpanxion for Bagel Brands<br>2021–2023</div><a href=\"#eb-story\" class=\"eb-story-link cursor-interaction\">The story behind the order ↓</a></div><figure class=\"eb-hero-phone\"><img src=\"/portfolio/media/0af9d561a19cbb2262d6.webp\" width=\"385\" height=\"837\" alt=\"Actual Einstein Bros. home screen with Order Now action\"><figcaption>THE MORNING ROUTINE / ACTUAL APP CAPTURE</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"eb-screen-study\"><div><span class=\"eb-label\">IN THE CUSTOMER’S HANDS</span><h2>A few taps.<br><em>A familiar morning.</em></h2><div class=\"eb-screen-buttons\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Explore actual app screens\"><button type=\"button\" data-eb-screen=\"order\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Order</button><button type=\"button\" data-eb-screen=\"rewards\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Rewards</button><button type=\"button\" data-eb-screen=\"product\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Product detail</button></div><p data-eb-screen-caption role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">Choose when and how to pick up an order.</p><span class=\"eb-small\">Actual interface captures; these screens illustrate the work.</span></div><figure class=\"eb-active-phone\"><img data-eb-screen-image src=\"/portfolio/media/44798b91f61f01b72f05.webp\" width=\"385\" height=\"837\" alt=\"Actual ordering pickup screen\"></figure></section><article id=\"eb-story\"><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-0\"><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-1\">Bagel Brands had an app. It had a store finder, a rewards card, and an ordering system that had been patched for years and was failing people at the moment they were hungriest. Rewards counted what you bought and did nothing with the count. Ratings were low, and the reviews said why.</p><figure><img src=\"/portfolio/media/de25f23d90939e11c969.webp\" alt=\"Select Mobile Screens\"><figcaption data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-2\">Select Mobile Screens</figcaption></figure><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-3\">I led UX at Xpanxion on the rebuild, working directly with the Bagel Brands product team. The brief was Einstein Bros. Bagels first, then take the results and apply to Bruegger's Bagels and Noah's New York Bagels.</p></section><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-1\"><div class=\"eb-morning\" data-morning-clock data-clock-state=\"waiting\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"7:40 in the morning\"><span class=\"eb-clock-digits\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7:40</span><span class=\"eb-clock-period\" aria-hidden=\"true\">AM</span></div><h2 data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-4\">The question</h2><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-5\">Before any screens, one question. What does a regular actually want from this app at 7:40 on a Tuesday morning?</p><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-6\">The answer was short. Reorder the usual, know the reward is there, get out. Everything else, the menu browsing, the gifting, the nutrition, is real but secondary. That answer set the priority for the whole build. The order flow and the rewards had to work together to create a more cohesive experience or nothing else mattered.</p></section><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-2\"><h2 data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-7\">What I designed and built</h2><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-8\"><strong>A new design system, in Figma, from the ground up.</strong> A component library that engineering could build against and that the other two brands could re-skin without redesigning. Type, color, spacing, and every state of every control, so that &quot;add a bagel&quot; looked and behaved the same in three apps.</p><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-9\"><strong>Ordering, rebuilt.</strong> The legacy flow was replaced with one that handled customization, favorites, and reorders without dead ends. We added features the old system couldn't carry, and we made the cart reliable.</p><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-10\"><strong>Rewards that apply.</strong> Points earned in an order, shown in the order, redeemable on the next order. The old app tracked. The new one pays out. This sounds like a small change. It was the change customers had been asking for in reviews.</p><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-11\"><strong>The store side.</strong> Alongside the customer app I designed a proof of concept for the admin interface stores would use to manage menus, deals, inventory, and location-specific details. Design only. It never went to build while I was on the project. But the case for it was the same as for everything else. The customer sees a menu that's accurate because a manager updated it that morning in a tool that made the process easy and accurate. The app is only as good as what the store can keep current.</p><figure><img src=\"/portfolio/media/eea5e1c64a0d344bab21.webp\" alt=\"Store admin proof of concept for menus, deals, and inventory\"><figcaption data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-12\">Store admin proof of concept for menus, deals, and inventory</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/portfolio/media/a5b4deeae555c3a0b88c.webp\" alt=\"Product Detail Admin\"><figcaption data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-13\">Product Detail Admin</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-3\"><h2 data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-14\">How it ran</h2><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-15\">Weekly standups with product managers, engineers, and the business analyst. I owned UX and UI end to end, brought flows to stakeholders with the tradeoffs already drawn, and adjusted with engineering when the platform pushed back. The design system meant those adjustments landed once and propagated everywhere.</p></section><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-4\"><h2 data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-16\">One decision I'd defend</h2><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-17\">Designing the store admin at the same time as the customer app, not after, even though it stayed a proof of concept. The argument against was obvious. Scope. The argument for was that a beautiful order screen showing an item the store ran out of at 9 a.m. is a broken promise. Fixing the data path fixes the experience.</p></section><section class=\"eb-chapter eb-chapter-5\"><h2 data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-18\">What happened</h2><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-19\">The app in the App Store today is the one we shipped, with the sprints of usability work that followed launch. It holds a 4.8-star rating across more than 120,000 ratings. The same system runs Bruegger's and Noah's.</p><p data-einstein-source=\"einstein-source-20\">The thing I'm proudest of isn't the number. It's that three brands with different personalities run on one set of shared components without looking like a template.</p></section></article><footer class=\"eb-footer\"><span>EINSTEIN BROS. BAGELS / JOSH COLE</span><a href=\"/#case-studies\" data-return-case class=\"cursor-interaction\">← Back to case studies</a></footer></main><script type=\"application/json\" data-eb-screens>{\"home\":\"/portfolio/media/0af9d561a19cbb2262d6.webp\",\"order\":\"/portfolio/media/44798b91f61f01b72f05.webp\",\"rewards\":\"/portfolio/media/64f6a2dae2c0fe1f2d47.webp\",\"product\":\"/portfolio/media/719855120e3dc7238e09.webp\"}</script></section>\r\n<section id=\"vrc-case-study\" class=\"xc-case xc-vrc\" hidden aria-label=\"VRC Suite case study\"><header class=\"xc-header\"><a href=\"/\" data-logo-home class=\"case-home-logo cursor-interaction\" aria-label=\"Josh Cole — home\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%2232%2061%20685%20378%22%20role%3D%22img%22%20aria-labelledby%3D%22