<a href=\"/#case-studies\" data-return-case class=\"cursor-interaction\">← Case studies</a><span>UST / RFP TRIAGE AGENT</span></header><main><section class=\"ac-rfp-hero\"><div class=\"ac-rfp-meta\"><span>UST / PUBLIC SECTOR</span><span class=\"ac-pilot-label\">WORKING PILOT · TESTING & TUNING</span></div><h1>The first pass.<br><em>The final call.</em></h1><div class=\"ac-rfp-split\"><div><span>01 / AUTOMATION</span><h2>Handle<br>the volume.</h2><p>Ingestion. Retrieval. Rubric. Scoring. Digest.</p></div><div><span>02 / PEOPLE</span><h2>Hold<br>the judgment.</h2><p>The rules. The edge cases. The pursuit decision.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-rfp-hero-bottom\"><p>UST RFP Triage Agent</p><span>Built end to end / 2024</span></div></section><section class=\"ac-rfp-walkthrough xc-pad\"><div class=\"xc-section-heading\"><span class=\"xc-eyebrow\">ILLUSTRATIVE WORKFLOW / BASED ON THE PILOT</span><h2>Follow a request.<br><em>See the handoffs.</em></h2><p>An explanation of the system’s paths, using generic scenarios.</p></div><div class=\"ac-rfp-scenarios\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Choose an illustrative request path\"><button type=\"button\" data-rfp-scenario=\"complete\" aria-pressed=\"true\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Complete request</button><button type=\"button\" data-rfp-scenario=\"login\" aria-pressed=\"false\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Source behind a login</button><button type=\"button\" data-rfp-scenario=\"stop\" aria-pressed=\"false\" class=\"cursor-interaction\">Hard-stop rule</button></div><p class=\"ac-rfp-description\" data-rfp-description aria-live=\"polite\">The full request is available. The system applies the team’s current rubric and prepares an auditable first pass.</p><ol class=\"ac-rfp-flow\" data-rfp-flow><li><span>01</span><h3>Request arrives</h3><p>Power Automate turns the email into a text file.</p></li><li><span>02</span><h3>Team-owned rubric</h3><p>The agent applies the current rules table.</p></li><li><span>03</span><h3>Score + store</h3><p>A spreadsheet records the rule results; a summary carries the score and flags.</p></li><li><span>04</span><h3>Teams digest</h3><p>The team receives a morning synopsis and recommendation.</p></li><li><span>05</span><h3>People decide</h3><p>Worthwhile requests receive a human review before pursuit.</p></li></ol><p class=\"ac-rfp-flow-note\">A human path in. A human decision out.</p><script type=\"application/json\" data-rfp-scenarios>{\"complete\":{\"title\":\"Complete request\",\"description\":\"The full request is available. The system applies the team’s current rubric and prepares an auditable first pass.\",\"nodes\":[[\"Request arrives\",\"Power Automate turns the email into a text file.\"],[\"Team-owned rubric\",\"The agent applies the current rules table.\"],[\"Score + store\",\"A spreadsheet records the rule results; a summary carries the score and flags.\"],[\"Teams digest\",\"The team receives a morning synopsis and recommendation.\"],[\"People decide\",\"Worthwhile requests receive a human review before pursuit.\"]]},\"login\":{\"title\":\"Source behind a login\",\"description\":\"Automatic retrieval stops at the access boundary. A person fetches the complete request and returns it to the same flow.\",\"nodes\":[[\"Request arrives\",\"The email does not contain the complete RFP.\"],[\"Retrieval is blocked\",\"A login or an access restriction prevents automatic collection.\"],[\"A person fetches it\",\"Someone logs in, copies or downloads the request, and returns it to the flow.\"],[\"Score + store\",\"The complete request is evaluated against the team’s current rubric and retained.\"],[\"Digest → decision\",\"The summary reaches Teams; people review and decide.\"]]},\"stop\":{\"title\":\"Hard-stop rule\",\"description\":\"A hard stop ends further rule checking. The rejection and its reason remain stored and visible to the team.\",\"nodes\":[[\"Complete request\",\"The agent has the request it needs to evaluate.\"],[\"Hard stop matches\",\"One of the team’s explicit disqualifying rules is met.\"],[\"Record the reason\",\"The no-go is retained for audit; the other criteria need no further checking.\"],[\"Teams digest\",\"The rejection reaches the same summary and delivery path.\"],[\"Human oversight\",\"The team can inspect what the agent turned away and why.\"]]}}</script></section><article class=\"xc-story\"><section id=\"ust-rfp-introduction\" class=\"xc-chapter xc-chapter-0\"><span class=\"xc-chapter-index\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01 / UST-RFP</span><h2>The brief</h2><p style=\"grid-row:2\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-1\">The public sector team at UST had a volume problem. Every morning, requests for proposals and quotes landed in the inbox from request systems, from scraping, sometimes direct. Roughly twenty a day. Some were potentially worth half a million dollars or more. And each one needed a decision. Is this worth pursuing, or not?</p><p style=\"grid-row:3\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-2\">That decision wasn't simple. It depended on what the team does and doesn't do, the type of project, which level of government it came from, conflicts of interest, whether UST actually had the skills the work required. A real rubric lived in the team's heads and in a checklist. Applying it to twenty requests a day, fast enough to matter, was more than anyone could keep up with.</p><p style=\"grid-row:4\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-3\">The question I asked before building anything. Which parts of this decision repeat the same way every time, and which need a person? The rubric repeated. The final call didn't. That split became the architecture.</p><p style=\"grid-row:5\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-4\">I built a system that does the first pass.</p></section><section id=\"ust-rfp-how-it-works\" class=\"xc-chapter xc-chapter-1\"><span class=\"xc-chapter-index\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02 / UST-RFP</span><h2 data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-5\">How it works</h2><p style=\"grid-row:2\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-6\">The team's decision criteria became a rules table. A structured, editable rubric the team owns. They add to it, trim it, and the agent always scores against the current version. The judgment stays theirs. The system just applies it at volume.</p><p style=\"grid-row:3\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-7\">Each morning's requests flow through Power Automate, which simplifies every email into a text file and hands it to a CoPilot agent. When an email doesn't contain the full RFP, which happens often, Power Automate scrapes the rest.</p><p style=\"grid-row:4\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-8\">Sometimes scraping hits a wall. A request gated behind a login, a system that blocks automated access. The system doesn't pretend that case away. It hands it to a person, who logs in, copies or downloads the full RFP, and pushes it back into the flow. A designed fallback, not a failure.</p><p style=\"grid-row:5\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-9\">Once the agent has the complete request, it scores it against every rule in the table. A couple of rules are hard stops. If one of them trips, the request is an instant no-go and nothing else needs checking. For everything else, the agent writes a per-request spreadsheet with each rule scored, stores it, and generates a summary document. A synopsis of the request, the results, a final score, and the flags behind it. Go, maybe, or no-go, with the reasons attached.</p><p style=\"grid-row:6\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-10\">A no-go doesn't disappear. Every request gets scored and stored, including the ones a hard stop rejected, so the team can go back and audit what the agent turned away and why.</p><p style=\"grid-row:7\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-11\">The summary lands in a Teams channel. The whole team scans it over coffee. A morning of senior-people judgment becomes a digest they can read in minutes, and the handful of requests actually worth pursuing get pulled out and scrubbed by humans before anyone commits real time.</p></section><section id=\"ust-rfp-the-hard-part\" class=\"xc-chapter xc-chapter-2\"><span class=\"xc-chapter-index\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03 / UST-RFP</span><h2 data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-12\">The hard part</h2><p style=\"grid-row:2\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-13\">The hard part wasn't the plumbing. It was trust.</p><p style=\"grid-row:3\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-14\">These are six-figure pursuit decisions. For a team to rely on an agent's read of an RFP, the agent has to be right in a way that holds up. Not in a demo, in production, every morning, on messy inputs. That takes real work. When I left UST the system was a working pilot, still in testing, still being tuned. That wasn't unfinished business. That was the business. You don't ship an AI system that influences where a team spends half a million dollars and call it done after the first good output. You calibrate it until the people using it would stake a decision on it.</p><p style=\"grid-row:4\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-15\">The other thing I took away was about the tools themselves. Working in Power Automate, the logic was sound but the experience of assembling it fought me the whole way. The machine sits at the center of these builders, not the person, which makes the build far more complicated than it needs to be.</p></section><section id=\"ust-rfp-what-i-built\" class=\"xc-chapter xc-chapter-3\"><span class=\"xc-chapter-index\" aria-hidden=\"true\">04 / UST-RFP</span><h2 data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-16\">What I built</h2><p style=\"grid-row:2\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-17\">I built the automation and the CoPilot agent end to end. The ingestion flow, the scraping and human-fetch fallback, the scoring logic, the spreadsheet and summary generation, the Teams delivery. The rules, the spreadsheets, and the final decisions stayed with the public sector team, where they belong. The system applies the team's judgment. It doesn't replace it.</p><p style=\"grid-row:3\" data-extended-source=\"ust-rfp-source-18\">That split is the point. Automation handles the volume and the messy inputs. People handle the judgment and the edge cases. A human path in when ingestion hits a wall, a human decision out when the digest lands. The agent triages so the team can decide. That's the only responsible way to put AI near a decision this expensive.</p></section></article><footer class=\"xc-footer\"><span>UST / RFP TRIAGE AGENT / JOSH COLE</span><a href=\"/#case-studies\" data-return-case class=\"cursor-interaction\">← Back to case studies</a></footer></main><dialog class=\"xc-inspector\"><div class=\"xc-inspector-toolbar\"><span data-xc-inspector-caption></span><button type=\"button\" data-xc-inspector-close class=\"cursor-interaction\">Close ✕</button></div><label class=\"xc-inspector-zoom\">Image scale <output data-xc-zoom-label>100%</output><input type=\"range\" min=\"100\" max=\"220\" step=\"10\" value=\"100\" data-xc-zoom aria-label=\"Image scale\"></label><div class=\"xc-inspector-pan\"><img data-xc-inspector-image alt=\"\"></div></dialog></section>\r\n<section id=\"andculture-case-study\" class=\"xc-case xc-andculture\" hidden aria-label=\"Andculture case study\"><header class=\"xc-header\"><a href=\"/\" data-logo-home class=\"case-home-logo cursor-interaction\" aria-label=\"Josh Cole — home\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%2232%2061%20685%20378%22%20role%3D%22img%22%20aria-labelledby%3D%22signature-title%20signature-desc%22%3E%0A%3Ctitle%20id%3D%22signature-title%22%3EJosh%20Cole%20signature%3C%2Ftitle%3E%0A%3Cdesc%20id%3D%22signature-desc%22%3EVector%20outline%20traced%20from%20Josh%20Cole's%20original%20handwritten%20signature.%20Irregular%20stroke%20edges%20retained.%3C%2Fdesc%3E%0A%3Cmetadata%3EDerived%20from%20josh-cole-art-logo.png%3B%20SHA256%2079df